The 2nd International Workshop on the Cultural and Social Dimensions of Digital Libraries will be held in conjunction with the 9th International Conference on Digital Preservation (iPRES 2011) on October 26th 2011 in Glasgow. The aim of the workshop is to bring together researchers and practitioners to discuss the social and cultural aspects of digital preservation, and to exchange views on how to capture, represent, and interpret digital objects and collections. The workshop is co-organised by the Arts and Humanities Research Councils Digital Humanities Programme, the Digital Curation Unit, and the Open University. It is being held in conjunction with iPRES 2011, the 9th International Conference on Digital Preservation, which is co-located with the Digital Heritage 2011 conference.

A digital library is an organized collection of digital objects with provisions for their management and for their access for various purposes. Digital objects are defined as self-describing resources that are addressable and manipulable on the Internet. They may be stored in a variety of formats and in a variety of locations, and can include digital text, images, audio, video, and other multimedia objects. The management of digital libraries comprises the creation, maintenance, and organization of the digital objects and their metadata, and the provision of services for their access. Access can be provided to the digital objects themselves or to information about them, or to a combination of both.
The social and cultural aspects of digital libraries have been the focus of a number of research projects. These include: the CULMINA project, which looked at the impact of the digital library on the intellectual life of a university community; the DELos project, which developed a system for managing and presenting digital library collections in an interactive and participatory way; the PANDAS project, which studied the impact of the digital library on the intellectual life of a community of scholars, and on the processes of scholarly communication; the SPIDA project, which looked at the impact of the digital library on the intellectual life of a community of scholars; the Interoperability of Cultural Heritage Institutions project, which looked at the use of metadata and other information about cultural heritage resources to enable their interoperability; the DELOS Network of Excellence on Digital Libraries, which focused on the development of the underlying principles, concepts, techniques and tools for the construction of digital libraries; the DELOS Digital Library Framework, which was developed to support the creation of digital libraries; the MUSE project, which developed a framework for the management and exploitation of cultural heritage resources;
